A trusted timestamp we cannot check is reported as a bad code signature.

The chain, measured end to end, six layers of it, and the label is wrong at the top:

  1. MoneyMoney calls SecStaticCodeCheckValidity on its own bundle.
  2. validateDirectory reaches verifySignature, which asks CMSDecoderCopySignerStatus.
  3. SecCmsSignerInfoVerifyWithPolicy verifies the signature and gets GoodSignature.
  4. It then verifies the UNAUTHENTICATED attributes, which carry Apple's secure timestamp
     countersignature, and that returns errSecTimestampNotTrusted, -67884.
  5. So it does `goto loser` WITH vs STILL SET TO GoodSignature, and returns SECFailure.
  6. CMSDecoderCopySignerStatus turns any nonzero return into kCMSSignerInvalidSignature. Its own
     comment says it assumes that: "assume that a nonzero vfyRtn means bad signature".
  7. verifySignature throws errSecCSSignatureFailed, -67061.
  8. The application tells its user the file seems to be damaged.

The code signature is FINE at every step. What cannot be checked is the TIMESTAMP, whose trust
evaluation needs Apple's timestamp CA as an anchor, and this container has no trust anchors and no
trustd.

These traces are what made that legible. Three of them exist because the earlier ones lied by
omission, and each lesson is worth more than the line that carries it:

  - REPORT ON EVERY EXIT. Reporting only on the success path made the failing call print nothing,
    which reads exactly like the call not happening.
  - TAG WHO. Two verifications are in flight at once, so adjacent lines belong to different calls
    and the log is not a timeline. The signer pointer pairs them.
  - AN INSTRUMENT COMPILED OUT IS SILENT IN THE SAME WAY A PASSING CHECK IS. The status this
    needed was already syslogged, inside `#if SECTRUST_VERBOSE_DEBUG`, which is off.

--- a/OSX/libsecurity_smime/lib/cmssiginfo.c
+++ b/OSX/libsecurity_smime/lib/cmssiginfo.c
@@ -567,6 +567,46 @@
     return SecCmsSignerInfoVerifyWithPolicy(signerinfo,NULL, digest,contentType);
 }
 
+/*
+ * CIDER_TRACE_SECITEM: WHICH way a CMS signature failed.
+ *
+ * Everything above collapses this to kCMSSignerInvalidSignature, so "the signature is invalid" is
+ * all a caller ever learns -- and an application shows that to its user as "this file seems to be
+ * damaged". The distinction that matters is right here and is thrown away: DigestMismatch means the
+ * content we digested is not what was signed, BadSignature means the digest matched and the
+ * public-key verification failed. Those have nothing in common as bugs.
+ */
+static const char *cider_cms_vs_name(SecCmsVerificationStatus vs)
+{
+	switch (vs) {
+	case SecCmsVSUnverified:		return "Unverified";
+	case SecCmsVSGoodSignature:		return "GoodSignature";
+	case SecCmsVSBadSignature:		return "BadSignature (digest matched, key verify failed)";
+	case SecCmsVSDigestMismatch:		return "DigestMismatch (content is not what was signed)";
+	case SecCmsVSSigningCertNotFound:	return "SigningCertNotFound";
+	case SecCmsVSSigningCertNotTrusted:	return "SigningCertNotTrusted";
+	case SecCmsVSSignatureAlgorithmUnknown:	return "SignatureAlgorithmUnknown";
+	case SecCmsVSSignatureAlgorithmUnsupported: return "SignatureAlgorithmUnsupported";
+	case SecCmsVSMalformedSignature:	return "MalformedSignature";
+	case SecCmsVSProcessingError:		return "ProcessingError";
+	default:				return "unknown";
+	}
+}
+
+static void cider_cms_report(const void *who, const char *where, SecCmsVerificationStatus vs)
+{
+	/* Non-EMPTY: our drivers export switches unset-as-empty. */
+	const char *v = getenv("CIDER_TRACE_SECITEM");
+
+	if (v != NULL && v[0] != '\0') {
+		/* WHO, because two of these can be in flight at once and adjacent lines then belong to
+		 * different calls -- the same trap as reading a log as a timeline. */
+		fprintf(stderr, "CIDER_CMS verify signer=%p exit=%s verdict=%d (%s)\n",
+			who, where, (int) vs, cider_cms_vs_name(vs));
+		fflush(stderr);
+	}
+}
+
